<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="FortniteVideoSoftware.App.Controls.PhoneFrameMockup"
             IsHitTestVisible="False">
    <!--
    ================================================================================================
    PORTRAIT PREVIEW GUIDE — COORDINATE SPACE IS *SOURCE* PIXELS (1920x1080), NOT OUTPUT PIXELS.
    ================================================================================================
    This control is an OVERLAY drawn on top of the live 16:9 video, so its unit space is the SOURCE
    frame. The centre column (720 units) is the "portrait brick wall": the slice of the source that
    survives the Portrait Canvas Trick (1280 internal width / (16/9) = 720 source px).

    ⚠️ THE BUG THAT WAS FIXED HERE — READ BEFORE CHANGING ANY NUMBER BELOW ⚠️
    The phone SCREEN used to be 720x1080 with 150-unit black bars drawn INSIDE it. Both numbers
    were wrong, because 150 is a measurement in the 1080x1920 OUTPUT space and this grid is in
    1080-tall SOURCE space. The consequences were:
      * the "phone" was 720x1080 = 2:3, which is the aspect of the VIDEO CONTENT AREA, not of a
        phone screen (a phone is 9:16);
      * the bars painted solid black over source rows 0-150 and 930-1080 — 300 of the 1080
        surviving rows, i.e. 27.8% of the picture — which are FULLY VISIBLE in the exported file.
    That made the preview contradict both the export and the zoom rubberband: the rubberband
    correctly let the user draw into those rows (they really are exported), while this overlay
    insisted they were dead space. Verified against the real filter chain
    (scale=3414:1920,crop=1280:1920:1066:0,scale=1080:1620,pad=1080:1920:0:150): source row Y maps
    to output row 150 + Y*1.5, so source 0..1080 -> output 150..1770. NOTHING in the source is ever
    hidden by the bars; they are pure added canvas.

    CORRECT GEOMETRY (all in source units, 720 = the surviving slice width):
      output px -> source units  =  720 / 1080  =  2/3
      screen height  1920 output px * 2/3 = 1280 units   (720x1280 = 9:16 ✓)
      bar height      150 output px * 2/3 =  100 units   (x2 = 200)
      video area                          = 1080 units   (100 + 1080 + 100 = 1280 ✓)
    The 1080-unit video band therefore aligns EXACTLY with the live video underneath, and the bars
    sit OUTSIDE it — which is why the screen deliberately overflows the 1080-tall grid by 100 units
    top and bottom (ClipToBounds="False" on both grids permits this). Everything inside a bar is
    scaled by the same 2/3 so the status/home indicators stay proportional.
    ================================================================================================
    -->
    <!--
    ================================================================================================
    MASK OVERFLOW — why the two side masks carry huge negative margins.
    ================================================================================================
    This whole mock-up lives in a Viewbox with Stretch="Uniform", so the 1920x1080 grid below is
    scaled to FIT its container and centred. Whenever the container is not exactly 16:9 — which is
    the normal case for the Granular editor's video pane and the Main App preview — that leaves
    pillarbox bars to the left and right (or letterbox bars above and below) of the scaled content.

    The masks used to be plain 600-unit columns, so they covered only as far as the scaled grid
    reached. The bars beyond it stayed BRIGHT: the centre dimmed correctly and the far left and
    right edges suddenly did not, which is exactly the reported symptom. Those outer bars are just
    as discarded by the portrait crop as everything else outside the 720-unit slice.

    Negative margins let each mask paint far outside the grid without changing the grid's measured
    size, so the Viewbox's uniform scale — and therefore every coordinate in this control — is
    completely unaffected. The overflow is bounded by the HOST, which clips: the Granular video
    Border and the Main App video Grid both set ClipToBounds="True". Do not "tidy" these margins
    away, and do not turn clipping back on for the Viewbox.
    ================================================================================================
    -->
    <Grid Width="1920" Height="1280" ClipToBounds="False">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="600" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="720" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="600" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <Border Grid.Column="0" Background="{DynamicResource AppPortraitMaskBrush}" />
        <Border Grid.Column="2" Background="{DynamicResource AppPortraitMaskBrush}" />
        <Grid Grid.Column="1" Width="720" Height="1280" ClipToBounds="False">
            <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppPhoneBezelRimBrush}" BorderThickness="4" CornerRadius="40" Margin="-4"/>
            <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppPhoneBezelBrush}" BorderThickness="6" CornerRadius="38" Margin="-2"/>
            <Border CornerRadius="32" ClipToBounds="True">
                <Grid>
                    <Border Height="100" Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneBezelBrush}" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                    <Border Height="100" Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneBezelBrush}" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" />
                    <!--
                    TEXT STRIP — MAPS 1:1 ONTO THE EXPORTED 150px BAND. DO NOT SHRINK IT.

                    The exporter overlays the text picture at 0,0 on the 1080x1920 canvas
                    (MobileFilterBuilder line 157), so it fills the whole 1080x150 top band edge
                    to edge. TextOverlayGenerator.GeneratePng always produces exactly 1080x150 —
                    both call sites use the defaults (MainWindow line 2137, ProcessWorker line
                    290) — and 1080/150 is 7.2, precisely the same shape as this 720x100 bar
                    (720/100 = 7.2). So filling the bar reproduces the export exactly, with no
                    distortion. Stretch="Fill" states that intent; because the two shapes match,
                    it behaves identically to Uniform while making a future size mismatch
                    obvious instead of silently letterboxing.

                    WHAT THIS REPLACED: Height="70" with Margin="0,28,0,0". Uniform then forced
                    the width to 70 x 7.2 = 504 units inside a 720-unit screen, so the preview
                    showed your text at 70% of its real width and pushed 28% down the band. That
                    ratio dated back to the original 720x1080 mock-up and survived the later
                    geometry fix unchanged, so it had never matched the export.

                    ZIndex: the decorative status bar below is declared after this image and
                    would otherwise paint over it. The real export has no status bar — the text
                    is the only thing in that band — so when long text wraps upward and reaches
                    the chrome, the text must win. That is what actually renders.
                    -->
                    <Image Name="PortraitImage" Width="720" Height="100" ZIndex="1"
                           VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           Stretch="Fill" Margin="0" />
                    <Grid Height="100" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="20,8,20,0">
                        <TextBlock Text="9:41" Foreground="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" FontSize="{DynamicResource AppFontSize28}" FontWeight="SemiBold" HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="0,4,0,0"/>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top" Spacing="24" Margin="0,5,0,0">
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="2">
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="3.5" Height="7" CornerRadius="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"/>
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="3.5" Height="10" CornerRadius="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"/>
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="3.5" Height="14" CornerRadius="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"/>
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeDimBrush}" Width="3.5" Height="18" CornerRadius="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="5G" Foreground="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" FontSize="{DynamicResource AppFontSize16}" FontWeight="Bold" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="5,0,0,0" />
                            </StackPanel>
                            <Grid Width="22" Height="18" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="0,0,0,2">
                                <Path Data="M 1,4 C 7,-1 15,-1 21,4" Stroke="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeDimBrush}" StrokeThickness="3" StrokeLineCap="Round" />
                                <Path Data="M 5,8 C 9,5 13,5 17,8" Stroke="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" StrokeThickness="3" StrokeLineCap="Round" />
                                <Path Data="M 9,12 C 10.5,10.5 11.5,10.5 13,12" Stroke="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" StrokeThickness="3" StrokeLineCap="Round" />
                                <Ellipse Fill="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="4.5" Height="4.5" Margin="0,15,0,0" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                            </Grid>
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Spacing="1" Margin="0,0,0,2">
                                <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeMutedBrush}" BorderThickness="2" CornerRadius="6" Width="46" Height="22" Padding="2">
                                    <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" CornerRadius="2.5" Width="18" HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
                                </Border>
                                <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeMutedBrush}" Width="4" Height="9" CornerRadius="0,4,4,0" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>
                    <Grid Height="100" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Background="{DynamicResource AppPhoneBezelBrush}">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="90">
                            <Path Data="M 18,0 L 0,11 L 18,22 Z" Fill="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="24" Height="22" Stretch="Uniform" />
                            <Grid Width="20" Height="20">
                                <Ellipse Stroke="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" StrokeThickness="2" Width="20" Height="20" />
                                <Ellipse Fill="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" Width="8" Height="8" />
                            </Grid>
                            <Rectangle Fill="Transparent" Stroke="{DynamicResource AppPhoneChromeBrush}" StrokeThickness="2" Width="20" Height="20" RadiusX="3" RadiusY="3" />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>
                </Grid>
            </Border>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>